I. INTRODUCTION
The unprecedented growth in industrialization,
urbanization, and transportation has led to a significant
increase in greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions, posing a grave
threat to the planet's climate and ecosystem. Accurate and
timely monitoring of GHG emissions is paramount for
formulating effective climate change mitigation strategies
and meeting international climate targets. Conventional
monitoring approaches, while useful, often suffer from
limitations in spatial coverage, frequency, and accuracy,
hindering real-time assessment and response to dynamic
emission patterns. In recent years, the emergence of the
Internet of Things (IoT) has revolutionized the way we
collect and process data from various physical and
environmental sources. The integration of IoT technology
with advanced sensor fusion techniques has shown immense
promise in environmental monitoring applications [1]. This
paper presents a pioneering research endeavor that harnesses
the power of IoT and sensor fusion to achieve real-time
estimation of GHG emissions, providing a significant step
forward in environmental sustainability efforts.
Motivation: The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate
Change (IPCC) reports unequivocally confirms that human
activities are the primary drivers behind the unprecedented
rise in GHG concentrations in the Earth's atmosphere.
Carbon dioxide (CO2), methane (CH4), nitrous oxide (N2O),
and fluorinated gases are among the major GHGs
responsible for the greenhouse effect, trapping heat and
leading to global warming. To combat climate change
effectively, international agreements, such as the Paris
Agreement, set ambitious targets to limit global warming
well below 2 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels.
Meeting these targets requires accurate, continuous, and
comprehensive monitoring of GHG emissions across diverse
sectors, including industrial processes, transportation,
agriculture, and energy production. Traditional monitoring
methods, such as stationary monitoring stations and satellite
based remote sensing, have proven valuable but often
struggle to provide real-time data or localized measurements
