II. LITERATURE REVIEW
The literature review presented in this section aims to
provide a comprehensive overview of existing research and
advancements related to real-time estimation of greenhouse
gas (GHG) emissions using IoT-integrated sensor fusion
techniques. The review is structured into three main themes:
a) IoT-based environmental monitoring, b) sensor fusion
methods in environmental applications, and c) GHG
emission estimation and monitoring technologies.
A. IoT-Based Environmental Monitoring: IoT
technology has gained significant attention in the realm of
environmental monitoring due to its potential to
revolutionize data collection, transmission, and analysis.
Various studies have explored the implementation of IoT
networks for monitoring air quality, temperature, humidity,
and other environmental parameters. For instance, Li et al.
[4] demonstrated the deployment of a low-cost IoT sensor
network for real-time air pollution monitoring in urban areas,
highlighting its potential in supporting pollution control and
management measures. Moreover, the integration of IoT
devices to monitor indoor CO2 concentrations, showcasing
the efficacy of IoT-based systems in tracking indoor GHG
emissions has presented [7].
B. Sensor Fusion Methods in Environmental
Applications: Sensor fusion is a key component in enhancing
the accuracy and reliability of environmental monitoring
systems. Researchers have investigated various sensor fusion
techniques to integrate data from heterogeneous sensors and
improve the estimation of environmental parameters. In a
study by Wang et al. [2], an adaptive fusion method was
proposed for integrating data from different weather sensors
to predict air quality with higher precision. Additionally,
Hong et al. [3] explored the use of sensor fusion algorithms
to combine data from ground-based and satellite sensors,
aiming to improve the monitoring of regional GHG
emissions.
C. GHG Emission Estimation and Monitoring
methods: A significant body of literature is dedicated to the
development of technologies for estimating and monitoring
GHG emissions. Satellite remote sensing techniques, such as
those employed in the Copernicus Sentinel missions, have
shown promise in providing global-scale GHG concentration
data. However, these methods often lack the resolution
required for localized and real-time estimations. In contrast,
ground-based monitoring systems, offer precise
measurements but may suffer from limited spatial coverage
and practical challenges in dense urban environments [9-12].
The literature review highlights the potential of IoT
integrated sensor fusion for real-time GHG emission
estimation and monitoring. By combining insights from IoT
